0 \\leq r \\lt n\n$$\n\nThis simple idea has far-reaching consequences, as we'll soon discover.\n\nAs an example, suppose that $a=56$ and $n=5$. Then, we can write\n\n$$\n56 = \\underbrace{{\\color{red}{11}}}_{q}\\cdot 5 + \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{1}}}_{r}\n$$\n\nWe can find $q$ using integer division. Once we know $q$, it's then straightforward to find $r$." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153365 #edb/ref 1065 4006 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153365 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "afc98e47-2d5e-5d68-8ebf-06da0e82b2fc" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153365 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585]}
